    "answer": {
      "short": "No solution.\n\nThe only algebraic candidate is $x=1$, which makes both original logarithm arguments zero.",
      "worked": "Both original arguments must be positive. The conditions $x^2-1>0$ and $x^2-x>0$ together give $x<-1$ or $x>1$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\log_2\\left(\\frac{x^2-1}{x^2-x}\\right)&=1\\\\\\frac{x^2-1}{x^2-x}&=2\\\\x^2-1&=2x^2-2x\\\\(x-1)^2&=0\\\\x&=1\\end{align*}$$\n\nAt $x=1$, both original arguments equal $0$, so neither logarithm is defined. The only candidate is invalid; therefore the equation has no solution.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
